And so in Scout Mastership in seven minutes or less,

68
00:05:09,000 --> 00:05:11,000
we'll be going back and taking a look at that.

69
00:05:11,000 --> 00:05:14,000
And then we have great email question that came in

70
00:05:14,000 --> 00:05:16,000
and that should be enough for this podcast.

71
00:05:16,000 --> 00:05:19,000
So let's get started, shall we?

72
00:05:19,000 --> 00:05:24,000
Scout Mastership in seven minutes or less?

73
00:05:32,000 --> 00:05:34,000
When we talk about youth leadership and scanning,

74
00:05:34,000 --> 00:05:38,000
we have to talk about it in the context of the age of the Scouts,

75
00:05:38,000 --> 00:05:40,000
the maturity of the Scouts,

76
00:05:40,000 --> 00:05:43,000
the experience of the Scout leaders

77
00:05:43,000 --> 00:05:47,000
and the experience of the Scout unit.

78
00:05:47,000 --> 00:05:49,000
Those are all very important things.

79
00:05:49,000 --> 00:05:52,000
Now, the basic principles of youth leadership.

80
00:05:52,000 --> 00:05:54,000
they will apply in every sense.

81
00:05:54,000 --> 00:05:58,000
but we need to remember that scouting and scouting principles

82
00:05:58,000 --> 00:06:01,000
is not a one size fits all.

83
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:04,000
It gets tailored to individual situations

84
00:06:04,000 --> 00:06:07,000
and that's part of the genius of the program.

85
00:06:07,000 --> 00:06:09,000
Youth leadership in a Scout troop

86
00:06:09,000 --> 00:06:11,000
has got to be developmentally based and progressive.

87
00:06:11,000 --> 00:06:14,000
All youth leadership is based on the same principles

88
00:06:14,000 --> 00:06:17,000
but they will not function exactly the same

89
00:06:17,000 --> 00:06:20,000
and our expectations have to be scaled

90
00:06:20,000 --> 00:06:23,000
to the age and the experience of our Scouts.

91
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:26,000
We don't start out, for instance, as a child

92
00:06:26,000 --> 00:06:30,000
with a two wheel 22 speed mountain bike right

93
00:06:30,000 --> 00:06:32,000
At the top of a really steep hill.

94
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:35,000
You know, I started out with a tricycle in the driveway

95
00:06:36,000 --> 00:06:41,000
and then I moved on to a two wheeler with training wheels

96
00:06:41,000 --> 00:06:44,000
and finally ended up pedaling away on my own

97
00:06:44,000 --> 00:06:47,000
on my Schwinn 10 speed bike,

98
00:06:47,000 --> 00:06:49,000
which, if you are a person of a certain age,

99
00:06:49,000 --> 00:06:52,000
you will realize the magic of the Schwinn 10 speed

100
00:06:52,000 --> 00:06:55,000
and you'll remember the excitement

101
00:06:55,000 --> 00:06:57,000
of getting that racing bike.

102
00:06:57,000 --> 00:07:00,000
So we're not going to start with a racing bike.

103
00:07:00,000 --> 00:07:03,000
Let's start with an understanding that scouting

104
00:07:03,000 --> 00:07:06,000
invests youth leaders with full responsibility

105
00:07:06,000 --> 00:07:09,000
for making things happen.

106
00:07:09,000 --> 00:07:11,000
It's not responsibility that's given to adults

107
00:07:11,000 --> 00:07:13,000
who then dull this out.

108
00:07:13,000 --> 00:07:16,000
The program really puts it right on the Scouts

109
00:07:16,000 --> 00:07:20,000
and the Scouts have this responsibility-

110
00:07:20,000 --> 00:07:23,000
we could say constitutionally-

111
00:07:23,000 --> 00:07:25,000
and the reason that that's important to remember

112
00:07:25,000 --> 00:07:29,000
is that the methods I'm about to explain-

113
00:07:29,000 --> 00:07:32,000
we need to progress away from adult involvement.

114
00:07:32,000 --> 00:07:35,000
as closely as I'm about to explain it,

115
00:07:35,000 --> 00:07:37,000
We also need to understand. Scouts

116
00:07:37,000 --> 00:07:40,000
get to make decisions and plans free from the coercion

117
00:07:40,000 --> 00:07:43,000
and interference of adults,

118
00:07:43,000 --> 00:07:46,000
but they are going to need their support

119
00:07:46,000 --> 00:07:48,000
and they are going to need their advice.

120
00:07:48,000 --> 00:07:53,000
So what I'd like to do is suggest a way forward

121
00:07:53,000 --> 00:07:56,000
in given a particular situation.

122
00:07:56,000 --> 00:07:59,000
let's say that you are a brand new Scout leader,

123
00:07:59,000 --> 00:08:02,000
you have a group of brand new Scouts

124
00:08:02,000 --> 00:08:05,000
who are 11 or 12 years old

125
00:08:05,000 --> 00:08:11,000
and you have just started a brand new troop.

126
00:08:11,000 --> 00:08:14,000
How do you go with youth leadership there?

127
00:08:14,000 --> 00:08:16,000
I mean, you don't have the experience,

128
00:08:16,000 --> 00:08:18,000
the Scouts don't have the maturity

129
00:08:18,000 --> 00:08:20,000
and experience to put this together.

130
00:08:20,000 --> 00:08:23,000
How are you going to be a youth led group?

131
00:08:23,000 --> 00:08:26,000
Well, I'm going to suggest

132
00:08:26,000 --> 00:08:29,000
it's best to begin with direct, simple choices.

133
00:08:29,000 --> 00:08:32,000
I wouldn't start with a long presentation

134
00:08:32,000 --> 00:08:35,000
about leadership based on a lot of abstract principles.

135
00:08:35,000 --> 00:08:38,000
It's not going to work for guys who are 11 or 12 years old.

136
00:08:38,000 --> 00:08:43,000
Work for some of them, but not for the great majority of them.

137
00:08:43,000 --> 00:08:47,000
What I think we're looking for is something more like:

138
00:08:47,000 --> 00:08:50,000
that's a tricycle, here's the pedals

139
00:08:50,000 --> 00:08:52,000
and this is how you make it go forward.

140
00:08:52,000 --> 00:08:54,000
This is how you steer it.

141
00:08:54,000 --> 00:08:56,000
Quick stuff.

142
00:08:56,000 --> 00:08:58,000
I get out the Scout Handbook.

143
00:08:58,000 --> 00:09:00,000
If you look at the current edition of the Scout Handbook,

144
00:09:00,000 --> 00:09:03,000
look at page 36 through 38.

145
00:09:03,000 --> 00:09:06,000
Skip over the types of patrols

146
00:09:06,000 --> 00:09:11,000
and you've got maybe half a dozen paragraphs there

147
00:09:11,000 --> 00:09:14,000
about the subject: your patrol.

148
00:09:14,000 --> 00:09:16,000
Let's say, you get those guys

149
00:09:16,000 --> 00:09:18,000
and you stand up in a circle

150
00:09:18,000 --> 00:09:20,000
and everybody has their Scout Handbook

151
00:09:20,000 --> 00:09:23,000
and each one of the boys reads a paragraph

152
00:09:23,000 --> 00:09:25,000
of that section of the book.

153
00:09:25,000 --> 00:09:27,000
You don't take a whole lot of time

154
00:09:27,000 --> 00:09:29,000
and explain and discuss things,

155
00:09:29,000 --> 00:09:31,000
You just read the paragraphs

156
00:09:31,000 --> 00:09:34,000
and then you can begin asking questions.

157
00:09:34,000 --> 00:09:37,000
So you guys, how big should a patrol be,

158
00:09:37,000 --> 00:09:39,000
Do you figure?

159
00:09:39,000 --> 00:09:42,000
So this is going to be a patrol. then

160
00:09:42,000 --> 00:09:44,000
Will that do for you?

161
00:09:44,000 --> 00:09:47,000
Do you need to divide up into two patrols?

162
00:09:47,000 --> 00:09:49,000
No, this is going to be a patrol.

163
00:09:49,000 --> 00:09:51,000
So now you need to elect a patrol leader.

164
00:09:51,000 --> 00:09:53,000
It says in the Scout Handbook

165
00:09:53,000 --> 00:09:55,000
that patrols have names and they have flags

166
00:09:55,000 --> 00:09:58,000
and all kinds of patches and stuff.

167
00:09:58,000 --> 00:10:00,000
So do you guys want to do that?

168
00:10:00,000 --> 00:10:02,000
Want to have a name and a flag and a patch?

169
00:10:02,000 --> 00:10:06,000
Okay, well, I'll be back in five minutes

170
00:10:06,000 --> 00:10:08,000
and tell me what the name and the flag

171
00:10:08,000 --> 00:10:10,000
and the patches are going to look like,

172
00:10:10,000 --> 00:10:13,000
what you're going to choose for a patrol name,

173
00:10:13,000 --> 00:10:17,000
And then that probably took 20 minutes or so,

174
00:10:17,000 --> 00:10:19,000
maybe 30.

175
00:10:19,000 --> 00:10:22,000
And you did that at a Scout meeting

176
00:10:22,000 --> 00:10:27,000
and that is probably about all you're going to do,

177
00:10:27,000 --> 00:10:30,000
because with 11 or 12-year-olds

178
00:10:30,000 --> 00:10:32,000
they're going to be very energetic

179
00:10:32,000 --> 00:10:34,000
and they're going to be very interested

180
00:10:34,000 --> 00:10:36,000
in playing a game right about then probably,

181
00:10:36,000 --> 00:10:38,000
Or doing something other than standing around,

182
00:10:38,000 --> 00:10:41,000
reading from a book and talking to you.

183
00:10:41,000 --> 00:10:43,000
So that's fine,

184
00:10:43,000 --> 00:10:46,000
You get 20 minutes, 15 minutes, 30 minutes, you know,

185
00:10:46,000 --> 00:10:48,000
depending on the group,

186
00:10:48,000 --> 00:10:50,000
the dynamics involved.

187
00:10:50,000 --> 00:10:54,000
And then let's get to the next meeting

188
00:10:54,000 --> 00:10:57,000
and the next time around

189
00:10:57,000 --> 00:11:02,000
the patrol leader is working right alongside you

190
00:11:02,000 --> 00:11:04,000
and they're reading another little section,

191
00:11:04,000 --> 00:11:07,000
pages 48 and 49.

192
00:11:07,000 --> 00:11:09,000
And that section is entitled

193
00:11:09,000 --> 00:11:10,000
Where Do You Want to Be.

194
00:11:10,000 --> 00:11:12,000
It'll only take a few minutes

195
00:11:12,000 --> 00:11:16,000
and if each boy reads a paragraph of the section,

196
00:11:16,000 --> 00:11:19,000
then they're not listening to you do it,

197
00:11:19,000 --> 00:11:21,000
They're actually participating in it

198
00:11:21,000 --> 00:11:23,000
and they're going to follow very closely

199
00:11:23,000 --> 00:11:25,000
when they know that they're going to read.

200
00:11:25,000 --> 00:11:28,000
So you read Where Do You Want to Be,

201
00:11:28,000 --> 00:11:30,000
And this explains all the great stuff

202
00:11:30,000 --> 00:11:33,000
that Scouts do in their troop and their patrol.

203
00:11:33,000 --> 00:11:34,000
Okay,

204
00:11:34,000 --> 00:11:37,000
And they read that

205
00:11:37,000 --> 00:11:40,000
and you say, okay, Mr Patrol Leader,

206
00:11:40,000 --> 00:11:45,000
let's make a list of five or 10 things

207
00:11:45,000 --> 00:11:47,000
that you guys would like to try.

208
00:11:47,000 --> 00:11:50,000
Now it might take, you know,

209
00:11:50,000 --> 00:11:52,000
broadly leading questions on your part,

210
00:11:52,000 --> 00:11:54,000
It might take some real digging,

211
00:11:54,000 --> 00:11:56,000
but in the end you're going to have a list

212
00:11:56,000 --> 00:11:57,000
of five or 10 things

213
00:11:57,000 --> 00:12:01,000
and there goes, your 15 or 20 minutes is over.

214
00:12:01,000 --> 00:12:03,000
That was a lot of hard work

215
00:12:03,000 --> 00:12:06,000
and the patrol is ready to do something else.

216
00:12:06,000 --> 00:12:08,000
They're ready to, you know, have a foot race

217
00:12:08,000 --> 00:12:10,000
or, do you know, run around

218
00:12:10,000 --> 00:12:12,000
and play a game or something.

219
00:12:12,000 --> 00:12:14,000
And then maybe you get back

220
00:12:14,000 --> 00:12:16,000
and for the last five or 10 minutes

221
00:12:16,000 --> 00:12:17,000
you say: okay, guys,

222
00:12:17,000 --> 00:12:19,000
put your hands together, out of these

223
00:12:19,000 --> 00:12:22,000
five to 10 things, pick two or three

224
00:12:22,000 --> 00:12:25,000
And once they have done that,

225
00:12:25,000 --> 00:12:27,000
take your favorite out of the two or three

226
00:12:27,000 --> 00:12:29,000
and take that home with you-

227
00:12:29,000 --> 00:12:30,000
and you have some work to do-

228
00:12:30,000 --> 00:12:32,000
Take that idea and develop it according to the method

229
00:12:32,000 --> 00:12:35,000
you'll find on pages 49 through 52

230
00:12:35,000 --> 00:12:37,000
of the Scout Handbook

231
00:12:37,000 --> 00:12:39,000
and then bring that back to the Scouts

232
00:12:39,000 --> 00:12:42,000
and explain it to them very quickly.

233
00:12:42,000 --> 00:12:44,000
This is where you want to get out of stopwatch.

234
00:12:44,000 --> 00:12:47,000
Now, that's meant to be literal.

235
00:12:47,000 --> 00:12:50,000
I mean a literal physical stopwatch

236
00:12:50,000 --> 00:12:52,000
and time yourself

237
00:12:52,000 --> 00:12:54,000
and take about 10 minutes

238
00:12:54,000 --> 00:12:56,000
at the very most, at the very most,

239
00:12:56,000 --> 00:12:57,000
and when 10 minutes is up,

240
00:12:57,000 --> 00:12:59,000
you just have to stop talking.

241
00:12:59,000 --> 00:13:00,000
So what you're going to talk about

242
00:13:00,000 --> 00:13:03,000
is you're going to go over

243
00:13:03,000 --> 00:13:06,000
what you did to develop the idea

244
00:13:06,000 --> 00:13:08,000
into a plan of action

245
00:13:08,000 --> 00:13:11,000
and you'll tell them how you use

246
00:13:11,000 --> 00:13:14,000
the information on pages 49 through 52

247
00:13:14,000 --> 00:13:17,000
of the Scout Handbook to do that.

248
00:13:17,000 --> 00:13:20,000
Show them that part of the Scout Handbook

249
00:13:20,000 --> 00:13:23,000
and then talk about how you developed the plan.

250
00:13:23,000 --> 00:13:26,000
You got maximum 10 minutes to do that-

251
00:13:26,000 --> 00:13:28,000
extra points if you can get it in less-

252
00:13:28,000 --> 00:13:31,000
and then ask who's going to develop the next idea

253
00:13:31,000 --> 00:13:34,000
and if that plan works,

254
00:13:34,000 --> 00:13:36,000
you know if they, like that plan,

255
00:13:36,000 --> 00:13:39,000
say okay, can we use this for our first camp out?

